Author: Eric Bolton <eric.didier.bolton@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 17:54:54 2026 -0400

    perf(vote): paint the block a press moves, in the press's own tick
    
    Pressing - or + took about a second to show anything. Measured with
    perf/vcast.mjs (it records the mousedown, the synchronous handler, the
    button's cast-state class, every block-votes broadcast that actually
    moves a block, the first WebGL draw after it, the POST and the WebSocket
    echo). It was two things, and neither was the request.
    
    The map waited for its own echo. castVotes was already optimistic, but
    only over EDGE counts - and on a map with a block layer the polygons are
    the heat and edges show only on hover. So the optimistic apply moved a
    number nothing on screen reads, and the first visible change was the
    server's delta landing with block counts: 53-105ms on localhost, a full
    round trip wherever the user actually is.
    
    So predict the block move instead. It is not the edge count and not
    their sum: a block counts a person ONCE per (vote type, direction)
    however many of its edges carry their vote (server block_votes.py, bd:
    HLEN), so the move is a presence boundary - a second press on the same
    corridor is 0, a flip is (+1 down, -1 up), extending onto more edges of
    a block I already hold is 0. planBlockVote now computes that alongside
    the plan it already derived, and applyMyBlockVoteChange is the
    increment twin of the delta path's SET. Getting the rule exactly right
    is what makes the confirming delta a no-op ("heat apply (diff): 0
    writes") rather than a visible correction; a vote that twitches reads as
    broken, which is worse than one that reads as slow.
    
    An optimistic write also has to survive until that echo. Two background
    refreshes walked over it: /api/my-votes -> resetMapVotes deletes rows a
    snapshot READ before the vote does not confirm, and /api/graph-votes
    installs a whole new count snapshot over graphDataRef. Neither could
    tell a speculative row from a settled one, so pendingVotes.ts makes that
    distinction explicit for the window between dispatch and echo. Both
    refresh paths re-apply what is still pending on top of server truth;
    the cast leaves the ledger when its own delta arrives, or on a 20s
    backstop so a dropped socket cannot shield it forever.
    
    Rollback now says so. A failure reverses the edge transitions AND the
    block move (recomputed against the store as it stands, not by negating a
    stale prediction), and raises the toast App.tsx already listens for -
    "Route not found" and the server's own refusals use the same one. It
    also refuses to fire when a LATER press on the same control has already
    re-written those edges: undoing then would revert the user's newest
    intent rather than this cast.
    
    Second finding, the one no amount of optimism fixes: a cast forced a
    full corridor re-cluster. scheduleOwnCastRecompute fires 400ms after
    every press and the epoch bump dropped every vote type's cached
    clusters - "20 corridors in 1215.0ms (9/9 types clustered over 9
    slices)", ~1.1s of long tasks landing right on top of the press. But a
    corridor for one type reads only that type's nets and the topology (the
    insight e3086cc built the cache on), and a cast changes one label. So
    the epoch now carries WHICH labels moved and drops only their clusters.
    perf/vcast-verify.mjs drives the check that matters: the incremental
    list is identical to what a cold load's full 9-type recompute produces
    in the same vote state.
    
    nyc-walkways, 61-edge/25-block corridor, localhost:
      click -> block heat   53-105ms -> 2.3-6.2ms (and now before the POST
                                        is even sent)
      click -> WebGL draw   68-118ms -> 25.9-39.2ms
      main thread blocked   1110-1126ms -> 51-59ms
      corridor recompute    1205ms (9/9 types) -> 65-73ms (1/9)
      cast-state highlight  22-28ms, already optimistic, unchanged
    Same shape on a point cast on nyc-proposals (1 block, 5.1ms, delta a
    no-op).
    
    Tests: optimisticVote.test.ts covers the prediction against the dedupe
    rule, the optimistic-then-authoritative no-op invariant, a wrong
    prediction being corrected rather than compounded, both refresh paths
    landing mid-flight (including one that lands after the POST but before
    the echo), and every rollback path including the superseded press.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

